WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:11.490
NIRAV MEHTA: Thank
you for joining me.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.530
My name is Nirav Mehta.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.320
I am Director of Product
Management for Google Cloud

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.490
Compute Engine.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.400
I'd like only 20 minutes
of your time today.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.700
The first half, I
want to share with you

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.330
some of our recent innovations,
but also the core principles

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.500
that drive them.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.940
And for the second half I'll
be talking with Omer Hasan, who

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.640
will join me from AppLovin.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.930
He's the VP of Operations.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.260
I'm very excited to have him
here for a short conversation.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.460
So let's get right
to it and start

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.160
with some of the investment
themes and principles that

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.060
drive us.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.540
First, Google Cloud
Compute Engine

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.540
is all about offering you
thoughtful and intuitive

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.920
choices.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.010
What this means is
it's easy for you

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.070
to select the right virtual
machine type, the right storage

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.870
type simply by looking at how
we've organized our choices

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.530
by workloads or by use case.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.050
And I'll talk about that
in a couple of minutes.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.690
Second, package
solutions that allow

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:21.190
you to very easily, in an
opinionated way, deploy,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:25.840
for example, SAP HANA, or
VMware, Microsoft SQL Server.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.750
We've thought through
reference architectures

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.870
and common deployment
templates to help

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.840
you get to outcomes quickly.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.890
Third, and perhaps
the most central tenet

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.380
that we have behind the
platform engineering,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.560
is simplicity at scale.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.170
Many customers I
talk with tell me

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.900
that Google Cloud is intuitive.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.490
And as they use it more,
they find it simpler,

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.190
and that we make complex
things seem simple.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.840
This is very important to us.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.680
We take it to heart and want
to ensure we maintain this

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.780
as we grow, add more features.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.889
So it's a commitment to
maintain this advantage,

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.580
and I'll talk
about how we do it.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.770
Security is paramount.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.980
Not only making sure that we
offer all the right security

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.980
controls and
compliance controls,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.360
but also that we put privacy
in the hands of the customer,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.305
or in the control of
you who owns the data.

00:02:28.305 --> 00:02:32.250
A simple example of this
is our recent feature

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.050
for encrypting
in-memory contents

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.320
within a virtual machine.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.960
The encryption keys are
controlled by the customer,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.760
and turning the
feature on is a matter

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.250
of simply checking one box.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.730
So simple security that's
comprehensive with the customer

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.000
remaining in control.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.510
And lastly, cost optimization
in all its forms,

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.390
not just offering the right
price points, the right price

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.070
performance points, but also
helping you reduce waste,

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:03.310
making machine learning based
recommendations that tell you

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.560
what types you should
be using, how you

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.690
should be adjusting your spend.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.540
All of that comes together
to make this a highly cost

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.110
optimized platform.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.120
So let's talk about
each of these,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.330
starting with VM families.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.290
With VM families, at
the very highest level,

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.810
there are two categories--
workload-optimized

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.010
and general purpose.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.940
For workload-optimized there
are three categories-- compute

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.090
optimized for high
performance computing,

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.260
and these are heavier on compute
power, or memory optimized,

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.990
or the M series of VMs.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:42.100
This is for the high-end
databases like SAP HANA,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.470
with very high
configuration of RAM.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.930
And lastly, there is
accelerator optimized,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.680
which is GPU-enabled
VMs for machine

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.420
learning training inference.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.830
I'll talk about a
couple of these soon.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.630
And looking at
general purpose, we

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.170
have, again, three
different categories,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.890
ranging from price-optimized
or cost-optimized,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:13.330
to price performance-optimized
for scale out.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.790
Our cost optimized family
is called the E-series,

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.860
or the Efficient series.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.950
If price per VM
or price per core

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.680
is the most important
consideration, that's the one

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:24.640
you would pick.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.830
If you want a
balance and a feature

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:30.310
set that works for the long
tail of general purpose

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.160
applications, you would
go for the M series.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.950
And the newest addition
to the VM family

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.490
is the T-Series, or the Tau VMs.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.430
These are optimized
with a lean feature set,

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.450
eliminating waste, and offering
only the right features

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.690
for scale out digital native
applications at a very

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.840
attractive price point.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.060
Specifically, we tested our Tau
VMs against competitive options

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.960
from leading clouds.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.530
And as you see here, we've
achieved a price point

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.740
that's 42% better
in price performance

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.930
than anything else
from a leading cloud.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:12.970
This includes anything with an
Intel, AMD, or ARM processor.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:16.060
We are committed to
all these CPU types.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.780
But what's interesting
is with Tau VMs

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:24.140
we've achieved the leading price
performance point with AMD VMs.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.780
That means your
developers don't have

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.780
to go port from
an x86 application

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.650
to ARM or another
type, and still

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:36.020
achieve this price performance.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.990
Tau VMs are in preview and we
are open for registration now.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:44.320
An example of recent innovation
for accelerator-optimized VMs

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.370
is our A2 VMs.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:52.230
These are the first VMs to
introduce the NVIDIA A100 GPU.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:57.030
And I'm very proud that we
have delivered the largest

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.590
single instance VM with up to 16
NVIDIA A100 GPUs among leading

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.950
clouds, so that you can bring
your machine learning training

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:09.330
workloads, achieve the scale
and performance you need,

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.373
and grow with Google Cloud.

00:06:11.373 --> 00:06:13.290
Another thing that's new
with virtual machines

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.610
is our spot VMs.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.370
Spot VMs are a new
and enhanced version

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:21.030
of our pre-emptible
VMs, which allow

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.120
you to use our
infrastructure when it is not

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.540
being used by us so that you
can get very attractive price

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:28.800
points.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.150
What's interesting here is we
guarantee 60% minimum savings,

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.390
which we believe is one of
the most compelling price

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.040
points in the market.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.180
Second, and more importantly,
this pricing is predictable.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.080
Quite often for this type of
VM you will see in the market

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.350
the prices vary
every five minutes.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.320
In our case, these
virtual machines

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.320
are much more
predictable in pricing.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.490
The variation is not more
frequent than once a month.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:03.480
Third, Kubernetes Engine
also supports smart VMs,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.420
so you can take advantage
of the price point

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.760
from the Kubernetes clusters.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.550
And in doing so,
we have made sure

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:16.140
that the preemption, when it
does happen, is very graceful.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.380
And lastly, you can
combine this price benefit

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.270
with custom machine types.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:25.020
Custom machine types allow
you to pick the exact point

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.560
for the number of
cores and memory,

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.340
so that you don't have any waste
that is often caused by having

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.690
predefined machine sizes.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.900
Moving on to block storage,
again, a simple set of choices.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.610
At the very left, you
are optimized for cost.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:47.160
We call it the persistent
disk efficient category.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.430
The efficient category
will be released next year.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.830
In the middle, as
you try to seek

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.080
a balance of price
and performance,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.600
we have PD balanced.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.220
And then, as you
go to the right,

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:02.950
you have the more
performance-intensive storage

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:07.630
options, PD performance
and PD extreme.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:11.500
For example, PD extreme would
be used for the highest end SAP

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:16.220
HANA databases, delivering
up to 1,200k IOPS.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.360
So we talked about
compute and storage.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.080
Let me address
simplicity at scale

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.700
with two features that
were released recently.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.220
And we are continuously
enhancing those features

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:30.510
with new capabilities.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:32.429
First is VM Manager.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:35.340
This is a suite of tools that
allow you to do three things--

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.799
patch management,
config management,

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:42.419
and inventory management
for operating systems.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:46.350
Combined together, this
allows very easy visibility

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.180
into the posture of your
operating system compliance.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.370
Quickly identify where
you are out of compliance,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.620
and then launch
from the dashboard,

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:58.610
or in an automatable
manner, the remediation

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:00.710
to bring it back to compliance.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:07.040
This is very important for
managing VM fleets at scale.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.970
Having this sort
of burden and toil

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.170
removed by having it
managed by the platform

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:16.590
saves a lot of time
and costly labor.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:20.130
Similar to VM Management,
we have this feature

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.780
called managed instance groups.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.900
But in this case, instead
of managing the VMs,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.390
you're actually
managing a group of VMs

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:30.210
as a composite application,
which is much more intuitive.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:31.860
If this is what
you want to do, you

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.840
can achieve that
with Google Cloud.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:39.460
For example, as shown here,
we can auto heal applications.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:42.240
So for example, some of the
VMs in a group stop working.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.310
The rest of the VMs
will take over the load.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:48.270
And when the VMs are healed,
they're put back into the group

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.750
and they start managing the
load with the rest of the group.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.990
We can also achieve autoscaling
and talk about that,

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.130
and things like auto updating.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:02.000
It's very important that the
managed instance groups are

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.870
stateful, so you can deliver a
stateful application, not just

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:07.640
stateless applications.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:08.970
We support that.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.310
So let's talk about autoscaling.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:13.880
With managed instance
groups you can scale up

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.230
and scale down as needed.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.100
Most often customers
in the past have been

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.260
using scheduled autoscaling.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.920
So if you're expecting
a peak at 9:00 AM, then

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.740
a little bit in advance of
that, schedule autoscaling.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:33.350
And then later in the day
have an autoscale down.

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:37.040
With Google Cloud you can
do another thing in addition

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.960
to schedule auto scale.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:45.500
You can use machine learning
to have the platform recommend

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:48.740
or automatically scale up and
scale down the managed instance

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.570
groups based on past
patterns of traffic.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.930
This is very useful when
you're in a business where

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:59.730
you don't quite know how your
traffic might evolve over time.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.060
And so using machine
learning at scale,

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:06.300
we are able to drive autoscaling
with managed instance groups.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.430
So in summary, I've covered a
few of these core principles

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:11.780
that drive us.

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:16.520
Hopefully you see how our
investments are really

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.120
driven by all the principles
that I've shared here.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.450
Over time I hope to come back
to you and share much more.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.520
And now I am very excited
to have with me Omer Hasan,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.280
VP of Operations at AppLovin.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:35.730
AppLovin is a leading
marketing platform

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:40.260
that helps mobile app developers
grow their applications.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:43.240
Omer, welcome, and thank
you for being with us.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:44.820
OMER HASAN: Glad
to be here, Nirav.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.990
NIRAV MEHTA: So Omer, let's
start with your migration

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.020
to Google Cloud.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:54.290
You achieved one of
the faster migrations,

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.630
closing down several
data centers,

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.130
and coming over to Google
Cloud in a very short time.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:05.060
I'd like to have you tell
us a little bit about how

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:05.900
you went about it.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:08.930
What were some of the
key tools, practices

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:12.410
that you used in achieving
the speed of migration

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:13.940
that you did?

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.550
OMER HASAN: Yeah, sure.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:20.330
Our journey did start over
on on-prem data centers.

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:24.800
And we found, as our
business continued to grow,

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.910
they no longer
could scale with us.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:32.300
And we looked at
Google Cloud for that,

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:37.280
and we leveraged tools like
Google Cloud Interconnect

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:40.640
to help with our
migration initially,

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:44.630
and then tools like
Terraform and Chef

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:49.550
to build up our infrastructure.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.800
We found that Terraform
was really simple

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.290
with Google Cloud, and leveraged
a lot of the APIs underneath.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:00.740
And we were able to build
our data centers really

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.690
fast and efficiently.

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:06.260
NIRAV MEHTA: What was
the rough timeframe?

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:11.002
And I think you did quite a few
data centers within a few days.

00:13:11.002 --> 00:13:11.960
OMER HASAN: Yeah, sure.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:15.980
We built out our infrastructure
first on Google Cloud.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:18.170
And when it came
to migration day,

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:21.710
we were confident enough
to do five data center

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:23.670
migrations in a single day.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.190
NIRAV MEHTA: Wow, very good.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:30.990
I'm sure a lot of planning,
but also a lot of skills,

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.750
thoughtful training
that went into it.

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:38.350
How did you go about
guiding your teams

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.890
to embrace a new concept
because they were working

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:43.480
with on-premise data centers?

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:46.242
What were some of the
observations there?

00:13:46.242 --> 00:13:47.200
OMER HASAN: Yeah, sure.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:52.600
Part of that was working
with Google's PSO team,

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:57.340
along with just reading
a lot of documentation

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:01.120
and understanding the
best practices of it.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.030
From there, we started
to automate and build out

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.960
our Terraform
scripts and modules.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:12.580
And it was easy from there.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:14.520
NIRAV MEHTA: So the
support for Terraform,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.680
having the right libraries
was important to you.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:20.220
You found the Google support
to be complete enough that you

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.050
could just be productive?

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.720
OMER HASAN: Absolutely.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:27.060
The Google team was
very responsive.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:31.120
And if we needed a new feature,
it was built out within a week.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:31.870
NIRAV MEHTA: Good.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.084
Good to know.

00:14:33.084 --> 00:14:36.700
With respect to once you
arrived on Google Cloud

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:40.270
and how you scaled up,
what benefits you saw,

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:41.890
can you start with
maybe, what were

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:46.030
some of the first observations
you had in, hey, this

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:49.270
is working a lot
better than it used to?

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.340
OMER HASAN: Yeah, the
impact was immediate.

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:57.910
We saw that in our
KPIs internally.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:01.480
We saw latency drop by 25%.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.340
We were just able to
handle a lot more.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:09.520
We saw, in addition to
that, a material impact

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:11.020
to the business.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.950
NIRAV MEHTA: What about SLAs?

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.870
How have your SLAs to
the business changed

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:20.290
as a result of this move?

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:23.680
OMER HASAN: We were able
to tighten up our SLAs.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.770
Given that things were
performing faster,

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.915
we were scaled out
more, and doing this

00:15:29.915 --> 00:15:31.540
all in the midst of
a growing business.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.420
And I'm pretty proud to say
that we were able to just

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:40.327
do more, and cheaper.

00:15:40.327 --> 00:15:41.410
NIRAV MEHTA: That's great.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:44.860
Yeah, that's what
we like to hear.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:49.450
Let's talk about the
network resilience,

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:51.400
the overall resilience
of the platform.

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.700
Where did you see advantages
in terms of eliminating

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.050
single points of failure?

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.760
OMER HASAN: Yeah, sure.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:05.300
When we were on-prem we would
have NAT-based load balancers.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.660
And we found with moving into
Google Cloud we didn't quite

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:10.170
need that anymore.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:14.090
And by eliminating that single
point of failure, and really

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.270
a choke point, we
felt more confident

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.670
in our infrastructure.

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:23.000
And particularly in
failover scenarios

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:29.790
where traffic increased by
a lot, or significantly,

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.628
we had an ease of mind there.

00:16:31.628 --> 00:16:32.420
NIRAV MEHTA: Great.

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:34.820
Yeah, good to know.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:38.540
I think the collection of
different availability features

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:42.020
come together to support
your applications.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:47.990
And knowing how you've used
those features to deliver scale

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.450
to your business is
very useful to us.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:56.090
It realizes the promise of
what we like to deliver.

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:58.220
I want to also talk
about things that you're

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:03.560
doing that are above the
raw virtual machine layer.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.050
Tell us about any
services you're using,

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:09.411
higher-order services in Google
Cloud beyond virtual machines.

00:17:09.411 --> 00:17:10.369
OMER HASAN: Yeah, sure.

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:13.911
We're using Dataproc today.

00:17:13.911 --> 00:17:15.619
NIRAV MEHTA: What are
you using that for?

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:17.452
OMER HASAN: We're using
that for our machine

00:17:17.452 --> 00:17:21.300
learning algorithms, and being
able to do that at scale.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.750
In fact, it was actually
one of the first services

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.460
we used at Google.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:29.390
And as we were growing
and scaling our business

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:35.330
and doing more ML, we found
that doing that on-prem

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:38.480
would just be a CapEx feat.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:45.330
And it would really take
a long time to build.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:50.310
And so when we saw Dataproc
it felt like a natural fit.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.120
And able to scale
out, and not only

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:54.980
scale out, but also
lower our SLAs,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.050
and do that in a
cost-efficient manner.

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.330
NIRAV MEHTA: Yeah, and a
lot more elastic, I imagine.

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:03.020
Because the nature of
these workloads is they

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:04.880
go up and down unpredictably.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:06.132
OMER HASAN: Correct.

00:18:06.132 --> 00:18:08.090
NIRAV MEHTA: So that's
what you meant by CapEx,

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:11.570
and not having to
spend on those peaks--

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:13.490
OMER HASAN: Exactly.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.160
NIRAV MEHTA: Makes
a lot of sense.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.670
So as I reflect
back, you migrated

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:23.940
almost the entire platform
over to Google Cloud,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:28.200
achieved a larger regional
footprint, better SLAs,

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.930
better performance,
cost savings.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.800
We couldn't ask for more.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:34.800
Thank you so much, Omer.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.470
That is an impressive journey.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.260
The speed with which you
migrated over to Google Cloud,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:43.590
how you scaled, how you took
advantage of all our principles

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.340
to improve your resilience,
offer better SLAs,

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.320
better performance,
these are all the things

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:52.330
we love to see in our customer.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:55.200
So thank you for taking
the time to join me

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:58.620
and sharing your experiences
with the audience.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:03.120
To all of you out there, it's
a real pleasure to be with you.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.910
Thank you for the time you took.

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.550
Hopefully you get a
good glimpse of what

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:10.760
drives our roadmaps, what
we've delivered recently.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:14.900
I hope to come back and share
much more with you over time,

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:17.840
and also bring more
conversations with customers.

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:19.660
Thank you.

